 in the endgame phase with only 4 empty squares remaining (B2, G7, G8, H8). We must choose the move that maximizes piece count and secures key structural advantages like corners.\n\n1.  **Move (G, 7)**: This is an X-square move adjacent to the empty corner H8. If you play here, you flip pieces on the diagonal (F6, E5). This creates a continuous line of your pieces ('@') from H8 to C3 ('*'). The opponent can immediately play H8, capturing the corner and flipping the entire diagonal back, resulting in a significant loss. This is a blunder.\n\n2.  **Move (B, 2)**: This is a safe, quiet move that flips only one piece (C3). While it doesn't give away a corner immediately, it is passive. In the endgame, passing up a secure high-value move usually allows the opponent to dictate the remaining sequence.\n\n3.  **Move (G, 8)**: This move places a piece on the edge in column 8.\n    *   **Captures**: It flips the vertical line A8-F8 (5 pieces) and the diagonal F7-E6 (2 pieces), totaling 7 flips.\n    *   **Safety**: Although G8 is a C-square next to the corner H8, the opponent cannot play H8 immediately after this move. To play H8, the opponent ('*') needs to sandwich your pieces against another '*'.\n        *   Vertically, the column becomes all '@' (A8 to G8), offering no anchor.\n        *   Horizontally, H7 is '*', so there are no captured pieces to flip.\n        *   Diagonally, G7 is empty.\n    *   **Strategic Outcome**: By playing G8, you secure the right edge. The opponent will be forced to play elsewhere (likely G7 or B2). If the opponent plays G7, you can then legally play H8 (capturing G7 and F6 diagonally), securing the corner and winning the local region.\n\nTherefore, (G, 8) is the best move as it scores highly, secures an edge, and leads to a winning endgame sequence where you capture the final corner.\n\nEvaluation: High value move. It secures the right edge (Column 8) by flipping 7 pieces and prevents the opponent from taking the corner H8, eventually allowi